      "correctIds": [
        "d"
      ],
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "text": "the virtual network settings for ta1",
        "explanation": "Technical Justification\nOption D (Correct): Configuring the virtual network settings for ta1 is the native, resource-level mechanism to enforce network isolation for Azure AI Services. This involves disabling public network access and creating\n\na Private Endpoint (or configuring Service Endpoints/Policies) within vnet1 that connects directly to the ta1 resource. This ensures the service is only reachable via a private IP address inside vnet1, providing granular, identity-based access control at the resource plane without requiring perimeter appliances.\nOption A (Incorrect): A Network Security Group (NSG) controls traffic flow to/from subnets or network interfaces within a VNet. It cannot restrict access to the public endpoint of a Platform-as-a-Service (PaaS) resource like ta1 from outside the VNet, nor can it create a private connectivity path. NSGs operate at L3/L4 and do not govern the Azure backbone routing to the service's public FQDN.\nOption B (Incorrect): Deploying an Azure Language service container moves the workload to a self-hosted environment (e.g., Azure Container Instances, AKS, or on-premises). This changes the deployment model entirely rather than securing the existing managed resource ta1. The requirement is to secure the specific managed resource instance, not to migrate to a containerized deployment.\nOption C (Incorrect): Azure Firewall is a managed, cloud-based network security service that protects VNet egress/ingress traffic (typically internet-bound or spoke-to-spoke). It operates at the network perimeter. It cannot prevent clients outside vnet1 from accessing the public endpoint of ta1 over the internet, nor does it provide a private link into the PaaS resource itself.\nReferences\nConfigure Azure AI services virtual networks\nCreate a Private Endpoint for Azure AI services"
      },
